  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{3}, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{6}+1, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$\n- It's continuous at $x=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "For $x=0,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)={0}^{6}+1=1$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{(x-1)}^{3}=(0-1)^{3}=-1\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{6}+1)={0}^{6}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ \n\nHence, I is false, II is true and III is false.",
